{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:4738",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine, a common topical anesthetic, is implicated in the interaction with the phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit beta through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that benzocaine acts as a target for several compounds, including K-252a and 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline, which in turn target the phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit beta. This indicates that benzocaine may influence the activity of this enzyme through the binding of these compounds, potentially modulating intracellular signaling pathways. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated. The involvement of this enzyme in cellular processes such as inflammation and apoptosis further supports the relevance of this relationship in biological contexts.",
